    {
      "object_id": "mathia_interpretation_61c622c61c20bf101f77de3ddd121acf46823b9fddd895f202043c0dcbb2ca75",
      "object_role": "interpretation",
      "proposed_content": "Repeated integration by parts converts differentiability of a compactly supported test into decay of its Mellin factor along vertical lines. In the smooth compact-support regime the source places that factor in a Paley-Wiener class, obtains rapid decay uniformly on fixed vertical strips, and states the needed L1 property. The representation therefore gains transform-side integrability by spending source-side regularity, but the lemma does not cover discontinuous tests and does not by itself perform Mellin inversion or a contour shift; exact quantitative bounds remain unavailable from the damaged displays.",
